---
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FeatureAtlas from '../../components/FeatureAtlas.astro';
import '../../styles/features.css';
---
<BaseLayout title="Feature atlas | David Blumenau" description="Source-backed feature stories about image revisions, agent streams, tool boundaries, memory and portable setup. Inspect compact examples and their evidence.">
  <main id="main" class="features-page">
    <section class="atlas-hero wrap" id="top"><a class="feature-back" href="/"><Icon name="arrow-left" /> The portfolio</a><div class="atlas-hero-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Studio / Engine / Connections / Memory / Workbench</p><h1>The work<br /><em>inside the work.</em></h1></div><div><p>Try another image direction without losing the first. Inspect a tool boundary. Recover the source of a half-remembered decision. The details are where the work becomes useful.</p><p>Thirty source-backed stories, with small examples you can inspect and evidence you can open. Choose a question, or filter the catalogue.</p><div class="atlas-featured-actions"><a href="/features/branching-workbench/">Try image branches <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a><a href="/features/native-tool-boundary-profiles/">Inspect a tool boundary <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a><a href="#atlas-heading">Browse all stories <Icon name="arrow-down" /></a></div></div></div></section>
    <section class="atlas-paths wrap" aria-labelledby="atlas-paths-title"><div class="atlas-paths-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Three ways in</p><h2 id="atlas-paths-title">Pick a useful question.</h2></div><div class="atlas-path-grid">
      <article><span class="atlas-path-number">01 / Recall</span><h3>Where did we work that out?</h3><p>Find one detail, load the relevant context, then follow connections across the archive.</p><ol><li><a href="/features/find-that/">FindThat</a></li><li><a href="/features/same-page/">Same-Page</a></li><li><a href="/features/knowledge-graph/">Knowledge graph</a></li></ol></article>
      <article><span class="atlas-path-number">02 / Trust</span><h3>Why should this fact stay?</h3><p>Resolve a name, inspect its source and keep a reasoned history of corrections.</p><ol><li><a href="/features/grounded-analysis/">Grounded analysis</a></li><li><a href="/features/personal-relevance/">Personal relevance</a></li><li><a href="/features/graph-curation/">Curation history</a></li></ol></article>
      <article><span class="atlas-path-number">03 / Workbench</span><h3>How does this work carry across sessions?</h3><p>Share the setup, give browser work an owner and return evidence for review.</p><ol><li><a href="/features/metacodex/">Portable configuration</a></li><li><a href="/features/browser-handoff/">Browser handoff</a></li><li><a href="/features/helm/">Helm</a></li></ol></article>
    </div></section>
    <div class="atlas-main wrap"><FeatureAtlas /></div>
    <aside class="atlas-source-note wrap"><p class="eyebrow">What the catalogue establishes</p><h2>Each story has a source boundary.</h2><p>These stories draw on inspected source, archived implementation discussions and selected recorded checks. Each story identifies the evidence behind it.</p><p>Some exhibits explain the design with invented examples. Others show actual product captures, saved model responses or recorded tool checks, often using fictional inputs. Their notes explain what ran and what you can do here. Source history and recorded checks do not establish today’s deployment. Private archive references stay in the research ledger.</p><a class="text-link" href="/world/">Explore the illustrated world <span aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></span></a></aside>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
